// Video checked by YouTube oEmbed: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2x47_jAkwrs (FLY8MA.com Flight Training, 2:37)
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).pilot=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.pilot.full||[]).push({
  num: 2, part: `Part One: The Share`, title: `The Machine`, weeks: `April to May`,
  tasks: [`I.B`,`I.G`,`II`,`IV`,`IX`,`XII`],
  cast: [
    [`Marisol Tran`,`Partner and A&P mechanic with an inspection authorization. Danny's closest friend at the airport. Keeps fixing things on Biscuit that aren't broken.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `The aviation medical examiner in Englewood worked out of a second floor office above a dentist, and the whole waiting room smelled faintly of mint and drill.`,
    `Jordan had filled out the MedXPress form the way she filled out a patient care report, every box, nothing rounded off. The doctor read it with his glasses pushed up on his forehead, listened to her heart for longer than she thought he needed to, and asked how she was sleeping. She told him the truth. Four hours, five on a good night, since March. He wrote something down, asked two more questions, and then asked a third that wasn't on any form: was she planning to fly tired. She said no. He looked at her the way she looked at drunk drivers who said they'd only had two, and then he signed.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="FAA medical certificate, third class">Name: JORDAN E. REYES. Class: THIRD. Limitations: NONE. This certificate shall be valid for the period prescribed in 14 CFR 61.23.</prop>`,
    `It was a piece of paper the size of an index card. She'd expected to feel something about it. Mostly she felt the same thing she'd felt in April, when the whole family had gone in for heart screening because that's what you do after a sudden cardiac arrest at 41. Echo, ECG, a cardiologist with cold hands who said "normal" like he was handing her a present. She'd driven home from that one and sat in the driveway for twenty minutes. This time she just put the card in her wallet behind her driver's license and went to get a burrito.`,
    `Her phone buzzed in the parking lot. Another number she didn't have saved. She was starting to think the airport had a phone tree.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Marisol Tran" time="2:12 PM">This is Marisol. I do the wrenching on Biscuit. Abby says you read the whole checklist. Saturday 8am, hangar six. Cowling's coming off for an oil change. Wear clothes you hate.</prop>`,
    `Jordan read it in the car. She knew the name. Danny had said it a few hundred times over the years, usually as a verdict. Marisol says it's fine. Marisol says absolutely not. At the funeral a woman with short gray streaked hair and a work jacket had stood at the very back, left before the reception, and sent flowers with a card that said only BISCUIT MISSES YOU TOO.`,
    `She texted back: <b>I'll be there.</b> Then, after a minute: <b>I don't own clothes I hate. I'll wear work pants.</b>`,
    `The reply came instantly. <b>You'll hate them by noon.</b>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Abby's Ground School: what's under the cowling`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Four strokes and two magnetos`,
        body: [`Marisol's got the cowling off, so we're doing this in the hangar today. Watch your head on the wing. Everybody hits it once. I've hit it twice. See the grease pencil drawing on her whiteboard? One cylinder, one piston, two valves, and an arrow that says SUCK SQUEEZE BANG BLOW. Honestly that's the whole engine. I'm not erasing it.`,
          `What you're looking at is a Lycoming O-320. Four cylinders lying flat, two a side, cooled by air, 160 horsepower on a good day, fed by a carburetor. Each cylinder does the same four things over and over. Intake, the suck: piston goes down, intake valve opens, fuel and air come in. Compression, the squeeze: valve shuts, piston comes back up. Power, the bang: plugs fire, the mixture burns, and the pressure shoves the piston down, which turns the crankshaft, which turns the prop. Exhaust, the blow: exhaust valve opens and the piston pushes the burned stuff out the pipe. Four cylinders, each on a different stroke, more than a thousand times a minute while you sit there drinking coffee.`,
          `Spark comes from those two boxes bolted on the back. Magnetos. Think of a magneto as a tiny generator with a permanent magnet in it, spun by the engine. It makes its own high voltage. It doesn't want anything from your battery or your alternator, ever. There are two plugs in every cylinder and each mag fires one of them, so lose a mag, or a plug, and you lose a little power. That's it. The key switch has five spots: OFF, R, L, BOTH, START.`,
          `On the runup you test each mag alone. BOTH to R, little RPM drop, back to BOTH, then the same thing with L. A small drop's normal and the POH says how much is allowed. Big drop or a rough engine, think fouled plug or a bad lead. No drop at all sounds great and isn't. It can mean the mag you just switched off isn't getting grounded, so it's live all the time. Oh, and Biscuit's tach reads fifty high. Doesn't matter here, you're reading a difference, so the lie cancels out.`,
          `Which is why every prop is a live prop. Master off, key sitting on the glare shield, who cares. The P lead is the ground wire between the mag and the switch. If it's broken and somebody moves the blade, the engine can fire. Marisol knows a guy. Ask her about him sometime, she'll tell you.`],
        terms: [[`Four stroke cycle`,`Intake, compression, power, exhaust. Every cylinder of a piston aircraft engine runs it.`],[`Magneto`,`A self contained, engine driven generator that makes the spark. It doesn't need the airplane's electrical system.`],[`P lead`,`The wire that grounds a magneto when the switch is off. If it breaks, the magneto stays hot.`],[`Mag check`,`Checking each magneto alone during the runup and comparing the RPM drop to the POH limit.`]],
        exam: `Know the strokes in order. Know that magnetos are independent of the electrical system, so an electrical failure doesn't stop the engine. Know that no drop on a mag check is a problem too.` },
      { h: `The carburetor, carb ice, and the mixture`,
        body: [`Get down here with me and look up under the engine. That's the carburetor, the float kind. Air rushes up through a skinny throat called the venturi, which drops the pressure, and fuel sprays in and evaporates, and between those two things the temperature in there can fall 60 or 70 degrees Fahrenheit. Got moisture in the air and the inside of that thing gets to freezing? Ice grows on the throttle valve and in the venturi. It chokes the fuel and air, you lose power, and if enough piles up the engine quits.`,
          `The PHAK puts it this way: most likely below about 70°F (21°C) with relative humidity over 80 percent, and still possible as warm as 100°F (38°C) with humidity down near 50 percent. Low power is where it gets you, like a long descent, throttle mostly shut and the engine cooling off. It can sit there and grow and you won't know until you push the throttle in and nothing much happens. People find that out on short final. Don't be people.`,
          `In a fixed pitch prop airplane like Biscuit, your first clue is the RPM sagging. Roughness may follow. Fix: full carb heat, that knob pulls warm air from around the exhaust into the carburetor. Warm air's less dense, so the RPM dips a little more when you pull it. With ice in there, the engine may run rougher for a while as the melt goes through, then the RPM comes back up. No ice, RPM drops and just sits. The worst moment is the moment it gets worse, and that's exactly when people shove it back in. Leave it on. All the way. Partial heat can make it worse. Carb heat also costs you power, up to 15 percent, so it's off for takeoff.`,
          `The mixture's the red knob. It sets how much fuel goes with the air, and the carburetor's calibrated at sea level, which we are very much not. Climb, the air thins, the fuel doesn't, the mixture gets richer. Too rich wastes gas, runs rough, and cakes your plugs with carbon. Centennial is 5,885 feet, so you start out high just sitting on the ramp. We lean Biscuit for taxi, and Cessna's procedure at a high field is to lean for max RPM during a full throttle runup before takeoff. Coming down, you push it back in, because too lean runs hot and invites detonation. That's the mixture going off like a firecracker instead of burning, and it can wreck an engine. Lower grade fuel than the airplane wants, high power with the mixture way lean, and overheating all push you that direction.`],
        terms: [[`Venturi`,`The narrow throat of the carburetor where air speeds up, pressure drops, and temperature falls.`],[`Carburetor heat`,`Heated air routed to the carburetor to prevent or melt ice. It reduces power and enriches the mixture.`],[`Mixture`,`The ratio of fuel to air. It gets richer as you climb unless you lean it.`],[`Detonation`,`Explosive, uncontrolled burning of the mixture. Caused by too lean, too hot, or the wrong fuel grade.`]],
        exam: `Classic questions: the conditions for carb ice, the first indication (RPM loss with a fixed pitch prop), what RPM does when you apply carb heat with ice present (drops, then rises), and what happens to the mixture in a climb (it gets richer).` },
      { h: `Fuel, oil, and electrical`,
        body: [`Biscuit's a high wing, so the fuel just runs downhill out of the wings into the carburetor. Gravity. There's no fuel pump to fail. That red handle on the floor, the famous sticky one, has LEFT, RIGHT, BOTH, and OFF, and the checklist wants BOTH for takeoff and landing. Every gallon that leaves a tank gets replaced by air coming in through the vents, so plug a vent and you can starve the engine while the wing's still half full. Every flight, you sample each tank drain and the strainer. Water, dirt, and color. Blue is 100LL. Anything else, you go find Marisol.`,
          `Oil keeps it cool and keeps it slippery. Oil pressure tells you right now when something's wrong. Oil temperature tells you slowly, like a friend who doesn't want to bring it up. High temp plus low pressure, together, you land.`,
          `Electricity runs the radios, lights, flaps, starter, fuel gauges, and the turn coordinator. Once the engine's running, an alternator bolted to it charges the battery and carries the load. Your master's split, BAT on one half and ALT on the other. The ammeter says whether the alternator's keeping up. Needle on the minus side, you're spending battery faster than you're putting it back. A steady discharge means the alternator isn't keeping up with the load, and the needle pinned all the way to the minus side means the alternator has quit. Full scale plus points at the voltage regulator. Either way, open the POH, turn off everything you don't need, and plan on landing, because now the battery is a clock.`,
          `Lose every bit of electrical power and guess what, the engine keeps running. Magnetos. You lose the radios, the lights, the electric flaps, and the fuel gauges turn into decorations.`],
        terms: [[`Gravity feed`,`A fuel system where the tanks sit above the carburetor and fuel flows down without a pump. Typical in high wing airplanes.`],[`Fuel vent`,`An opening that lets air into the tank as fuel leaves. A blocked vent can stop fuel flow.`],[`Ammeter`,`A gauge showing whether the alternator is charging the battery or the battery is carrying the load.`]],
        exam: `Expect a center zero ammeter question. Full scale minus is the alternator, full scale plus is the regulator. And know that an electrical failure doesn't stop a magneto engine.` },
      { h: `Pitot static instruments and the gyros`,
        body: [`Six gauges on the panel. Three run on air pressure. Airspeed compares ram air from the pitot tube, the little tube sticking out under the left wing, against static pressure from the static port, that tiny hole on the side of the fuselage you'd never notice. The altimeter and the vertical speed indicator use static only.`,
          `Pitot blocked but the drain hole's open, airspeed drops to zero. Opening and drain both blocked, the airspeed indicator turns into a bad altimeter: higher as you climb, lower as you descend. Static port blocked, the altimeter freezes wherever it happened, the VSI flatlines at zero, and airspeed lies, reading low when you climb above that altitude and high when you go below it. Some airplanes have an alternate static source inside the cabin. Cabin air's at slightly lower pressure than outside, so on alternate static the altimeter reads a bit high, the airspeed a bit fast, and the VSI jumps up for a second. No alternate static? Break the glass on the VSI. Cabin air gets into the system. You lose the VSI, which, fine.`,
          `The other three are gyros. In Biscuit the attitude indicator and the heading indicator spin on air sucked through them by an engine driven vacuum pump, and the suction gauge tells you that's happening. The turn coordinator's electric, on purpose, so a single failure doesn't take out all three. Gyros have rigidity in space, which the attitude and heading indicators lean on, and they precess when you push on them, which is how the turn coordinator works. Friction makes the heading indicator wander, so you reset it to the compass about every 15 minutes, straight and level, not accelerating.`,
          `Attitude indicator slowly leaning over like it's tired, heading indicator won't sit still? Look at the suction gauge. Low means the vacuum system is quitting on you. Turn coordinator, airspeed, and altimeter all still work. Fly those.`],
        terms: [[`Pitot tube`,`Measures ram air pressure for the airspeed indicator.`],[`Static port`,`Measures outside air pressure for the altimeter, VSI, and airspeed indicator.`],[`Rigidity in space`,`A spinning gyro's tendency to stay pointed in the same direction.`],[`Precession`,`A gyro's reaction to a force, felt 90 degrees around in the direction of rotation.`]],
        exam: `Blocked static: frozen altimeter, zero VSI. Blocked pitot with blocked drain: airspeed acts like an altimeter. Alternate static: everything reads a little high. Vacuum drives the attitude and heading indicators in most trainers.` },
      { h: `Airworthiness: the paperwork that makes the airplane legal`,
        body: [`Now the boring part that grounds airplanes. Before you fly, certain documents have to be aboard, and the memory aid is AROW. A: the airworthiness certificate, displayed where passengers or crew can read it. That's 91.203. R: the registration certificate, also 91.203, and it now expires seven years after the last day of the month it was issued, per 47.40. O: operating limitations, meaning the approved flight manual or POH, the placards, and the instrument markings, under 91.9. W: the weight and balance data for this exact airplane. Older books say ARROW. The extra R was the FCC radio station license, and now you only need that if you're flying to another country.`,
          `Then the inspections. Those live in the maintenance logbooks, not in the airplane. The annual has to be done within the preceding 12 calendar months, 91.409(a). Calendar months means you go to the end of the month: an annual signed on August 14 is good through August 31 the next year. Only an A&P with an inspection authorization, or certain repair stations, or the manufacturer, can do an annual. Marisol has her IA, which is why she gets to sign Biscuit's.`,
          `The 100 hour inspection, 91.409(b), applies when the airplane carries anyone for hire who isn't crew, or when someone gives flight instruction for hire in an airplane that person provides. Read that last part twice. You can go over the 100 hours by up to 10, but only to fly to a place where the inspection can be done, and the extra time comes out of the next interval. An annual counts as a 100 hour. A 100 hour doesn't count as an annual.`,
          `The ELT runs on two clocks, both in 91.207. It has to be inspected within 12 calendar months of the last inspection. Its battery has to be replaced when the transmitter's been used for more than one cumulative hour, or when 50 percent of the battery's useful life has expired, and the new expiration date gets marked on the outside of the transmitter and written in the records. The transponder needs a test every 24 calendar months before you can use it, 91.413, and here at Centennial, under the Denver Class B and its Mode C veil, you need it. You need ADS-B Out there too, 91.225, in the Mode C veil and under the Class B. The altimeter and static system test, every 24 calendar months, is required to fly IFR in controlled airspace, 91.411. For an airplane that only flies VFR it isn't legally required, though almost every owner gets it done with the transponder check. Airworthiness directives are mandatory, and the records have to show the status of each one, 91.417.`,
          `The owner or operator is responsible for keeping the airplane airworthy and the records straight. You're an owner now, so that's partly you. And as pilot in command, you confirm it before the wheels move.`],
        terms: [[`AROW`,`Airworthiness certificate, registration, operating limitations, weight and balance.`],[`Calendar month`,`Runs to the last day of the month. A 12 calendar month inspection done on any day of August is good through August 31 the following year.`],[`Airworthiness directive`,`A mandatory FAA notice to fix an unsafe condition in an aircraft, engine, propeller, or appliance.`]],
        exam: `Annual: 12 calendar months. 100 hour: only for hire, and the 10 hour overfly counts against the next one. ELT: 12 calendar month inspection, battery at 1 cumulative hour or half its useful life. Transponder: 24 calendar months. Altimeter and static system: 24 calendar months, for IFR in controlled airspace.` },
      { h: `Required equipment, and flying with something broken`,
        body: [`For day VFR, 91.205(b) lists what has to be installed and working. Pilots remember it as TOMATO FLAMES: tachometer, oil pressure gauge, manifold pressure gauge (for an altitude engine), altimeter, temperature gauge (for a liquid cooled engine), oil temperature gauge (for an air cooled engine), fuel gauge for each tank, landing gear position indicator (for retractable gear), airspeed indicator, anticollision lights (only for small airplanes certificated after March 11, 1996), magnetic compass, ELT (the mnemonic keeps it, but the requirement comes from 91.207, not 91.205), and seat belts and shoulder harnesses. Night adds FLAPS: fuses (a spare set, or three of each kind, that you can reach in flight), a landing light (only if you're operating for hire), anticollision lights (every airplane, at night), position lights, and a source of electrical power for everything installed.`,
          `When something's broken and the airplane has no MEL, and Biscuit doesn't, 91.213(d) is your process. First question: is it required? Check four places. The VFR day type certification requirements. The airplane's equipment list, or its Kinds of Operations Equipment List. 91.205 or any other rule for this flight. And any airworthiness directive. If any of those requires it, you're not going. If none of them do, the item gets removed, or deactivated and placarded INOPERATIVE, and any maintenance that takes gets logged under Part 43. Then a certificated pilot or a qualified mechanic decides it isn't a hazard. If it can't be deferred and can't be fixed where you are, a special flight permit can get the airplane to a shop.`,
          `Legal to defer doesn't mean smart to defer. One broken thing is a placard. Three broken things is a pattern.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"And four broken things is a phone call to me,"</span> from under the wing. She's right about that too.`],
        terms: [[`TOMATO FLAMES`,`The memory aid for the day VFR equipment in 91.205(b).`],[`FLAPS`,`The added night VFR equipment in 91.205(c).`],[`91.213(d)`,`The process for flying with inoperative equipment in a small piston airplane without an MEL.`],[`Special flight permit`,`FAA permission to fly an airplane that doesn't currently meet airworthiness requirements, usually to a repair shop.`]],
        exam: `The test asks which items are required for day VFR, and what the steps are to fly with something inoperative without an MEL: not required, deactivated or removed, placarded INOPERATIVE, and determined not to be a hazard.` }
    ],
    video: [`2x47_jAkwrs`,`Carburetor Ice in a Cessna: what you need to know`,`FLY8MA.com Flight Training`,`2:37`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f2s1`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.G`, title:`Treat it like it's live`, q:`With the cowling off for an oil change, how should Jordan treat Biscuit's propeller?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Hangar Six was the sixth door in a long row of corrugated steel hangars on the north side of the field, and inside it Biscuit sat with her nose naked. The cowling lay on a moving blanket like a shell somebody had cracked open. Under it the engine was smaller than Jordan expected, four fat finned cylinders laid flat, two on each side, wires and tubes everywhere like the inside of a ten year old's model kit.`,`Marisol was on a rolling stool with a drain pan and did not get up. She was shorter than Jordan, with forearms like rope and a pencil behind each ear. A radio on the workbench was playing classic rock, volume at about two. On the back wall of the hangar was a whiteboard, half covered in block capitals Jordan knew from a lifetime of birthday cards. DUE LIST. ANNUAL AUGUST 2026. TRANSPONDER OCTOBER 2026. ELT BATTERY MARCH 2026. It hadn't been touched in a while. There was a calendar next to it from a parts supplier, still on March. A coffee can of spare fuel caps sat on the shelf under the board.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"You're Jordan."</span> Not a question. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Okay. Lesson one. Go turn the prop for me, slowly, so you can feel the compression in each cylinder."</span> She went back to the oil filter.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Check that the key is out of the ignition and lying on the seat, then turn it.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The key's up on the glare shield where somebody left it. She turns the prop, and the compression comes back through the blade like a slow pulse, which is a cliché, and also exactly what it feels like. From the stool, Marisol: <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Key out's good. Key out's not enough."</span>`,
          why:`Key out is step one. But the switch only grounds the mags through the P leads, and if one of those wires is broken, the mag's hot no matter where the key is. You treat that blade like it bites.`},
        {t:`Turn it right away. The master's off and the battery is unplugged for the oil change.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-5},
          after:`Marisol's hand lands flat on the blade before Jordan's does. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"The battery doesn't run the spark. The engine does."</span>`,
          why:`Magnetos make their own electricity. The battery and the master switch have nothing to do with them. People lose hands believing that. Not in my hangar.`},
        {t:`Ask whether the mags are off and grounded, and still stay clear of the prop arc.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`Marisol stops wiping her hands on the rag. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Mags off. Key's up on the glare shield. I checked both P leads before you got here, and I'll still yell if you put an arm through that arc."</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Who taught you that?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"YouTube. Also a guy on B shift named Hector, who has nine fingers and tells everybody why."</span> Marisol almost smiles. Jordan stands off to the side and walks the blade around a few inches at a time, and the cylinders push back against her palm one after another, slow and stubborn, twice every turn.`,
          why:`Right. Magnetos don't need the battery, so a broken P lead means a hot mag even with the key out. Confirm it's grounded, then still handle the prop like it's live. Both, every time.`},
        {t:`Grab a blade with both hands and swing it hard, so she can feel the strokes better.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"No."</span> Same tone Jordan uses on I-225 when the guy from the rollover tries to stand up and walk it off.`,
          why:`Swinging a prop is how you hand start an engine. If that mag's hot, it starts, and you're standing where the blade goes. Slow, body out of the arc, never wrap your fingers around the blade.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s2`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.G`, title:`The venturi`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Marisol pulled the air box off the bottom of the engine and handed Jordan the carburetor's guts to look at, more or less. A throat, a float bowl, a butterfly valve on a shaft. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Air comes up through here and speeds up, pressure drops, fuel sprays in and evaporates. Same thing as an aerosol can getting cold in your hand. It can drop the temperature in there forty, fifty, sixty degrees."</span>`,`She tapped the butterfly valve with a screwdriver. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Ice grows right here. Your brother found that out the hard way, once. Ask him about it."</span> She heard what she'd said a second after she said it. The screwdriver stopped moving. Then she cleared her throat. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"So. Quiz. Which day do you worry about this most?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`A clear, dry January morning at 15°F, when everything is frozen solid already.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Cold, sure. Dry, though. Not much water in that air to make ice out of."</span>`,
          why:`Carb ice needs moisture. Very cold, very dry air doesn't carry much. The book's chart runs from about 20°F up to 100°F, and the worst of it sits under 70 with humidity over 80 percent.`},
        {t:`A muggy 90°F afternoon, cruising at normal power with the windows cracked open.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Could happen. Book says up to a hundred degrees. Not my top pick, though."</span>`,
          why:`It's possible on a hot humid day, and the book says so, so I won't mark you wrong. But high power keeps the throttle open and the engine hot. The day that scares me is cooler, wetter, and pulled back.`},
        {t:`A cool, damp 60°F morning after rain, coming down at low power for a while.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Marisol points the screwdriver at her. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"That one. Every pilot thinks ice means winter. It's sixty degrees and wet, throttle mostly closed, engine cooling off. That's when it grows and you don't notice till you push the throttle in and nothing happens."</span>`,
          why:`Under 70 degrees, humidity high, low power in a descent. That's the bull's eye on the PHAK chart, and it's sneaky because nothing feels cold. Carb heat on before you pull the power back.`},
        {t:`Only when it's below freezing outside and there's already ice building on the wings.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Nope. That's airframe ice. Different problem, worse day."</span>`,
          why:`The carburetor makes its own cold. The temperature drop happens inside, so you can get carb ice on a warm day with clear skies. Waiting to see ice on the wings means you're waiting for the wrong thing.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s3`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.G`, title:`Black plug`, q:`What does a dry, sooty plug say about how Biscuit's engine was run?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Marisol had all eight spark plugs out in a tray with numbered holes, a habit, she said, from a guy who once put them back in the wrong cylinders and spent an afternoon learning humility. She held one up to the light. The business end was crusted black and dry, like a match somebody blew out.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"This one's from the bottom of number three. Tell me why it looks like that."</span>`,`Jordan thought about the discovery flight. Abby taxiing out, the long wait behind a Cirrus at the runup area, the mixture knob all the way in the whole time because nobody told her to touch it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Mixture too lean. The engine ran hot and burned the plug black.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Other end of the dial. Lean runs hot and clean. Too hot, and you get detonation, which looks a lot worse than this."</span>`,
          why:`Lean burns hotter, not dirtier. Sooty black carbon means there was more fuel than the air could burn, which is rich.`},
        {t:`The plug is old and worn out. Replace it and the problem goes away.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"I'll clean it and gap it, sure. It'll be black again in a month if you keep taxiing the way everybody taxis."</span>`,
          why:`Cleaning or swapping the plug fixes the plug. It doesn't fix the cause. The cause is the mixture knob.`},
        {t:`Running full rich on the ground up here. At 5,885 feet that's too much fuel.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Yep. Carburetor thinks it's at sea level. It isn't. Lean it for taxi, lean it for the runup, and do what the POH says before takeoff up here."</span> She drops the plug in the cleaner. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Danny never leaned for taxi. I'd find these every oil change and yell at him, and he'd bring me a coffee, and we'd do it again."</span>`,
          why:`Thinner air, same fuel, richer mixture. Rich runs cooler, doesn't burn completely, and leaves carbon on the plugs. At a high field you lean on the ground and enrich as you descend.`},
        {t:`Bad fuel. Someone put a lower grade in the tanks than Biscuit is rated for.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Lower grade fuel gives you detonation and a very bad day. This is just soot."</span>`,
          why:`Wrong fuel is a real problem, and it shows up as detonation and high temperatures. This plug is telling you something boring: too rich.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s4`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.G`, title:`The mud dauber`, q:`What should Jordan do about what she found under the left wing?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Marisol sent her to walk the airplane while the oil drained. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Everything you looked at Saturday with Abby, look at again. I'll know if you didn't."</span>`,`Under the left wing, behind the strut, the little bent fuel vent tube hung down like a soda straw. Jordan crouched to look up into it. It was packed with something gray and papery. When she tapped it, a wasp walked out, looked at her, and flew away unbothered.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Leave it. The fuel caps have their own vents, so this one doesn't matter.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"You know which caps are vented and which aren't on this airplane?"</span> Jordan doesn't. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Then it matters."</span>`,
          why:`As fuel leaves the tank, air has to come in or the tank pulls a vacuum and the fuel stops flowing. You don't get to guess which openings the airplane needs. A blocked vent is a no go until it's clear.`},
        {t:`Fly it. If fuel stopped flowing, the fuel gauges would show it right away.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"The gauges show what's in the tank. Full tank, no flow, gauge reads full. Engine quits anyway."</span>`,
          why:`Fuel gauges measure quantity, not flow. The scariest version of this is a full tank, a happy gauge, and an engine that's starving.`},
        {t:`Poke the nest out with a bit of safety wire herself, then go on with the walkaround.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It comes out in pieces. Marisol watches from the doorway. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Fine. Now tell me it's all out, and tell me how you know."</span> Jordan can't.`,
          why:`Clearing it is the right instinct, and plenty of pilots do exactly that. But you have to know it's clear, all the way, and the person who maintains the airplane should hear about it. Mud daubers build deep.`},
        {t:`Show Marisol, and treat Biscuit as grounded until the vent is cleared and checked.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`Marisol crouches beside her, looks up the tube, and swears quietly in two languages. She clears it with a wire and blows air through until Jordan, standing at the fuel cap, can hear it whistle. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"That's the third one this spring. They love this airplane."</span> She writes it on a squawk sheet. Then she writes the date.`,
          why:`Blocked vent, no fuel flow, quiet engine, usually right when you least want it. Show the mechanic, clear it properly, prove air moves through it. Then write it down so the next person knows to look.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s5`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.G`, title:`The battery is a clock`, q:`The ammeter sits left of center at night over Limon. Which response is right?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By ten thirty the new oil was in and Marisol was safety wiring the oil filter. She finished it, looked at it for a while, snipped it off with a pair of dikes, and started over. The first one had looked perfect to Jordan, a tight little braid. She kept that to herself. Out on the ramp a fuel truck backed up with that beeping that never stops. Somebody out there was trying to start a Cherokee and it wasn't going well.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Next quiz, since you're just standing there. Night flight, you're over Limon on the way home, and the ammeter needle slides left of center and sits there, a steady discharge. What's happening, and what do you do?"</span>`,`She didn't look up from the wire. Jordan was getting the idea that this was what Marisol sounded like when she liked you.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ignore it for now. The battery is probably just charging after the engine start.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Charging shows plus, a little, right after start. You're an hour from the start and it's sitting on the minus side."</span>`,
          why:`Charging is the plus side. A steady discharge means the battery is carrying everything by itself, and it won't for long.`},
        {t:`The alternator has quit. Shut off what she doesn't need and plan to land soon.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Yep. Engine doesn't care, the mags have it. Radios and lights care a lot. Landing light off, second radio off, call Denver while you can still talk to them, and go find a runway before that battery's dead."</span>`,
          why:`A steady discharge means the alternator isn't carrying the load. The engine runs fine on the magnetos, but the battery is now a clock. Shed the load, check the POH, talk to ATC early, land at the nearest suitable airport.`},
        {t:`The engine is about to quit, so pick a field and set up for a landing right now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Why would the engine quit?"</span> Jordan opens her mouth. Closes it. <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"Right. Magnetos."</span>`,
          why:`An electrical failure doesn't stop a magneto engine. Landing in a field at night because of a dead alternator trades a manageable problem for a dangerous one.`},
        {t:`Turn the whole master off to save the battery, and fly home in the dark.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Saves the battery. Also turns off your radio, your lights, and the only way anybody knows you're there."</span>`,
          why:`Saving the battery is the right idea. Turning everything off at once is too blunt. Shed what you don't need and keep the radio and the lights you need to be seen and talk.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s6`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.G`, title:`Tape on the port`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Marisol had washed Biscuit on Friday, which explained why she was the cleanest thing in the hangar. On her second lap around the airplane, Jordan found a square of blue painter's tape stuck neatly over the static port on the side of the fuselage, a little hole she'd only learned the name of a week ago.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol" data-tone="Gruff, plain, matter-of-fact.">"Good. I tape it when I wash. Takes it off before anybody flies."</span> She said it with complete confidence. Then she added, more quietly, <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Usually."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Say it stayed on. You take off. What do the instruments do?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Only the airspeed indicator would be wrong. The pitot tube under the wing handles altitude.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Backwards. Pitot is ram air, for airspeed. The static port feeds three instruments."</span>`,
          why:`The pitot tube feeds the airspeed indicator only. The static port feeds the altimeter, the VSI, and the airspeed indicator. Tape over it and all three go wrong.`},
        {t:`The altimeter freezes, the VSI sits at zero, and the airspeed reads wrong in the climb.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"That's it. Altimeter stuck at field elevation. VSI dead flat. Airspeed reading low as you climb, because the trapped pressure behind it is higher than outside."</span>`,
          why:`Blocked static: the altimeter freezes where the blockage happened, the VSI reads zero, and airspeed is wrong. It reads low as you climb above that altitude and high as you descend below it.`},
        {t:`Nothing much. Biscuit's alternate static source would take over by itself.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Alternate static is a knob. Nothing about it happens by itself."</span>`,
          why:`Where an alternate static source is installed, the pilot selects it. And on it, the altimeter and airspeed read a little high, because cabin pressure is lower than outside.`},
        {t:`The altimeter would freeze, so she'd just use the GPS altitude to fly the pattern.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Altimeter, yes. And the other two? You'd be flying a pattern with an airspeed indicator that's lying to you."</span>`,
          why:`You got the altimeter, and a GPS altitude is a real backup. But the static port feeds the airspeed indicator and the VSI too. Miss that and you're trusting the wrong airspeed on final.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s7`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.B`, title:`Nobody reads those`, q:`Marisol says the whiteboard has everything that matters. What should Jordan do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At noon they ate gas station sandwiches on overturned buckets. The square of blue tape from the static port was stuck to Jordan's kneeboard now. Marisol had peeled it off and pressed it there herself. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol" data-tone="Gruff, plain, matter-of-fact.">"Keep that. It's a good reminder."</span> Jordan asked where the maintenance logbooks were.`,`Marisol pointed her sandwich at a fireproof file box on the workbench. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Airframe, engine, prop. Fifty years of other people's handwriting. Everything that matters is on the board."</span> She meant Danny's whiteboard. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol" data-tone="Gruff, plain, matter-of-fact.">"Nobody reads those cover to cover. Owners never do."</span>`,`At work, Jordan read every line of every run report she signed, including the ones she wrote, because the time you skim is the time a dose is wrong. She looked at the box. She looked at the board, which hadn't been touched since March.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Trust the whiteboard and Marisol. She's the mechanic, and keeping track is her job.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`She finishes her sandwich. The box stays closed. The board says what it says.`,
          why:`The owner is responsible for keeping the airplane airworthy, and the pilot in command checks before flight. Trusting the mechanic is fine. Not looking for yourself isn't.`},
        {t:`Look up N4791E on the FAA registry website and check that everything is current.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`The registry says Biscuit's registration is valid and lists the four partners, including a man who died in March. It says nothing about inspections. She closes the tab.`,
          why:`The registry shows registration, and it's worth knowing when that expires. It doesn't track inspections. Those live in the logbooks.`},
        {t:`Read through the logbooks herself and build a due list from the entries.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Knock yourself out."</span> Jordan opens a legal pad and starts with the most recent annual, then works backward, one line at a time: date, tach time, what was done, who signed. Marisol goes back to the airplane, then comes back to look over her shoulder twice, pretending to need a wrench from the bench.`,
          why:`Read the source. The logbooks are the record, and the board is just someone's copy of it. Build your own list and compare. A copy only helps if somebody's still reading it.`},
        {t:`Check the latest annual entry in the logbook, and trust the board for the rest.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The annual is clean, signed, last August. She closes the book. It feels like enough.`,
          why:`The annual is the big one, so checking it is smart. But the ELT, the transponder, and any recurring ADs all run on their own clocks. Spot checking the biggest item misses the small ones.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s8`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.B`, title:`03/2026`, q:`Jordan has found something overdue that's been on the whiteboard all along. How should she raise it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`It took her an hour and forty minutes. The annual: August 14 last year, signed by Marisol with her IA number. The transponder and static system checks: October two years ago, at the avionics shop on the south side. The ELT inspection: done with the annual. All of it matched the board.`,`Then a three year old entry for an ELT battery replacement, with a line at the end in Marisol's neat capitals: NEW BATTERY EXPIRATION MARCH 2026. Jordan got up, walked to the tail, and opened the access panel where Marisol had shown her the ELT that morning. The label on the side of the transmitter said the same thing. REPLACE BATTERY MARCH 2026. It had been on the board all morning, too. ELT BATTERY MARCH 2026. She'd read it at eight and walked right past it.`,`It was May. Danny had died in March. Out in the hangar, Marisol was singing along to a classic rock station, badly, and fixing the fuel selector detent for the second time that day.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Say nothing. Marisol did the annual. She must already know about it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`She puts the logbook back. Two days later Russ books Biscuit for a trip over the mountains.`,
          why:`If you found it, you assume nobody else has. Silence is how a small miss turns into a big one.`},
        {t:`Post it in the partners' group chat so all four owners see it at once.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Russ replies in eleven seconds with three skull emojis. Marisol reads it on her phone across the hangar, and Jordan watches her face go still.`,
          why:`The owners do need to know, so the instinct's right. But the person who keeps the records was twenty feet away. Tell her first, in person. She's the one who can fix it.`},
        {t:`Text Walt first, since he's the club safety officer and has to know.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Walt replies: <b>Have you told Marisol?</b> She hasn't. <b>Tell Marisol.</b>`,
          why:`Walt should hear, and he will. But you go to the person who can fix it and keep the records first. Going around her makes a records problem into a people problem.`},
        {t:`Show Marisol the logbook entry and the ELT label side by side, dates and all.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`Marisol reads the logbook line. Then the label. Then the logbook line again. She doesn't say anything for long enough that the song on the radio ends. Then she says, very calmly, <span class="said" data-who="Marisol" data-tone="Very calm, tight, quietly furious.">"I wrote that. I wrote it right there in my own handwriting, and I told him to put it on the board, and he did."</span> She sets the logbook down too carefully. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol" data-tone="Very calm, tight, quietly furious.">"And then I stopped looking at the board, because he always looked at the board."</span>`,
          why:`Just the facts and the dates, to the person who owns the records, in person. No blame in it. You'd be amazed how fast that gets a problem fixed.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s9`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.B`, title:`Can it fly Tuesday`, q:`Marisol's replacement part arrives Monday. What does that mean for Tuesday's lesson?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Marisol was on the phone with the parts counter at the shop across the field within two minutes. They'd have a battery Monday. She hung up and kicked the rolling stool across the hangar, not hard, and then went and got it and sat on it.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Russ flew it to Steamboat in April. Over the Divide. With a dead ELT battery, and I'm the one who should've caught it."</span> She wasn't talking to Jordan, exactly.`,`Jordan had a lesson with Abby on Tuesday morning. Her shifts didn't move. She asked the only question she had.`,`By then Marisol had put it in the partners' chat. Russ answered first.`,`<prop type="chat" from="Russ Kim" time="1:58 PM">wait. i flew it over the divide last month. with an expired elt battery. cool. cool cool cool. marisol i'm buying you a beer and also never flying again</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Fly Tuesday as planned. The annual is current, so the airplane is airworthy.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"The annual was current last month too, when Russ flew it over the mountains."</span>`,
          why:`A current annual doesn't make everything else current. The ELT has its own rules in 91.207, and the battery is one of them.`},
        {t:`Placard the ELT inoperative under 91.213(d), then fly wherever they want.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Marisol shakes her head before Jordan finishes the sentence.`,
          why:`You can't defer something a rule requires. 91.213(d) says the item can't be required by 91.205 or any other rule in Part 91, and 91.207 requires the ELT. A placard doesn't change that. 91.207(f)(10) does let you remove the ELT for repair and fly up to 90 days with a records entry and an ELT NOT INSTALLED placard, but that's a removal, not a 91.213(d) deferral.`},
        {t:`Fly Tuesday's lesson under 91.207's exception for local training within 50 NM.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Abby, on speaker, says, <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"There's an exception like that. I know it. I'm not using it to save one day on a hundred dollar problem."</span>`,
          why:`91.207(f) does exclude training flights that stay within 50 nautical miles of the airport, so it's a real argument. But the fix arrives Monday, and nobody wants to fly an airplane that's legal only on certain days. Fix it first.`},
        {t:`Wait for the new battery, with the new date marked on the ELT and in the logbook.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`Marisol nods once. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Monday. I'll swap it, run the test, mark the new date on the case and in the book. You fly Tuesday."</span> She stands up, walks to the whiteboard, and draws a box around the ELT battery line without erasing it.`,
          why:`91.207(c): when half the battery's useful life is gone, it gets replaced, and the new expiration goes on the outside of the transmitter and in the maintenance record. One day of waiting and the airplane is right everywhere it goes.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s10`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`I.B`, title:`Who provides the airplane`, q:`Which answer to Walt holds up under 14 CFR 91.409?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Walt came by at three, as promised, for the first of his monthly logbook checks. He read Jordan's pilot logbook, all 0.9 hours of it, with his glasses on the end of his nose, initialed the bottom of the page, and then listened to Marisol tell him about the ELT battery without interrupting once.`,`When she finished he said, <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"That's on all four of us."</span> Then he picked up Jordan's legal pad and read her due list. At the bottom she'd written: 100 HOUR, DUE AT TACH 2,441.8? WHY? She'd circled WHY twice.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Dry, patient, unhurried.">"Go ahead,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Walt" data-tone="Dry, patient, unhurried.">"Tell me why you think Biscuit gets a 100 hour."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It's required by law because Abby is being paid to give her instruction.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Half of it. Read the rest of the sentence."</span>`,
          why:`Paid instruction is one half of 91.409(b). The other half is that the instructor provides the airplane. Abby doesn't. You do.`},
        {t:`It's not required by law here, but the club agreement requires one anyway.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Walt takes his glasses off. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Correct. Abby's paid, but she isn't providing the airplane. You are, as an owner. So the law says annual only. I wrote the 100 hour into the agreement in 2009, because four people fly this thing and Abby teaches in it, and I don't like finding out about things at the annual."</span> He hands the pad back. <span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Good question to circle."</span>`,
          why:`91.409(b) covers carrying people for hire, and flight instruction for hire in an airplane the instructor provides. Jordan's a co-owner paying an independent CFI, so it doesn't apply by law. The club's agreement does, so the club tracks it like it does.`},
        {t:`It's never needed. The annual covers everything, so the club can skip it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"The law doesn't require it here. The agreement you signed does. Read your agreement."</span>`,
          why:`Not legally required here is not the same as not required. Partnerships can and do set stricter rules, and you agreed to them.`},
        {t:`Any airplane with more than one owner needs one, because the costs are shared.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Walt">"Splitting the bills isn't flying for hire. Nobody's paying anybody to ride."</span>`,
          why:`Co-ownership isn't carriage for hire. The 100 hour turns on carrying people for hire or giving instruction for hire in an airplane the instructor provides.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s11`, domain:`Preflight`, task:`II`, title:`Twenty minutes`, q:`They're short on time this morning. How much of the checklist should Jordan run?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday came in gray and damp. It had rained overnight and the ramp was still dark with it, puddles in the tie down rings, the windsock limp. Biscuit had a new ELT battery, a new date in marker on the transmitter, a new line in the logbook in Marisol's capitals, and a fresh box drawn on the whiteboard.`,`<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="7:41 AM">10 min out. Tower closes the north runway at 9 for paint, so we're on a clock. Be ready to start when I get there.</prop>`,`Jordan had the checklist in her hand. Marisol had been through every inch of this engine on Saturday. The oil was brand new. The cowling had been off and on and signed for.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Skip the items Marisol worked on Saturday. She's an A&P, and she signed for them.`, s:0, flag:`rushedpreflight`, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`She skips the oil, the cowling fasteners, and the static port, and is buckled in when Abby walks up. Nothing turns out to be wrong. Abby looks at the clock, then at her. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Fast."</span> It isn't a compliment.`,
          why:`Marisol's sign off means the work was done right on Saturday. Your preflight is about today. Mechanics are human, and the one who taped the static port told you so herself.`},
        {t:`Run the checklist from memory to save time. She's done it three times now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`She gets most of it. The fuel sample from the strainer isn't in her memory. Abby finds it missing in about four seconds.`,
          why:`Three times isn't a habit yet. Read and do. Memory is for emergencies, and even then you check it against the list afterward.`},
        {t:`Run the whole checklist, everything Marisol touched included, even if Abby waits.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:5},
          after:`Oil on the dipstick, the cap tight. Every cowling fastener. Static port clear, no tape. Fuel vent clear. Three cups of fuel, blue all the way down. It takes twenty two minutes and Abby waits for six of them, drinking coffee and saying nothing. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Find anything?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Jordan">"No."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Good. That's what a preflight usually finds. You do it for the time it doesn't."</span>`,
          why:`The preflight is the pilot's check, every flight, no matter who touched the airplane last. A clock is a reason to start early, never a reason to skip. If the lesson runs short because the preflight ran long, fine.`},
        {t:`Do the full walkaround, but trust Saturday's oil entry instead of pulling the dipstick.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Everything checks out. Abby pulls the dipstick herself on her lap around, reads it, and slides it back without comment. The without comment is the comment.`,
          why:`You did almost everything. But a logbook entry says what went in on Saturday, not what's in there now. Pull the dipstick. It takes ten seconds.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s12`, domain:`Navigation and emergencies`, task:`IX`, title:`Rough`, q:`After a low power descent on a humid morning, the engine loses RPM and shakes. What should Jordan do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`They took off on 35R and the door popped at rotation, like always. Jordan reached up and latched the top before Abby could open her mouth, and Abby snorted into the intercom. Out east the plains had gone green overnight, the air smooth and heavy as soup. The ATIS said thirteen degrees Celsius, dewpoint ten. She'd written that on her kneeboard right next to the strip of blue tape.`,`Abby had her fly a long slow descent at low power, 7,500 down to 6,500, then level off. Jordan pushed the throttle in. The RPM came up about halfway and quit coming. Then Biscuit started to shake, an ugly lopsided shudder through the floor and up into the yoke, a washing machine with one wet towel in it.`,(s.flags.rushedpreflight||G.rushedpreflight)?`For one bad second all she could think about was the dipstick she hadn't pulled.`:`For one bad second all she could think about was the phone call from Claire in March.`,`Abby didn't touch anything. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Your airplane. Talk to me."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Pull carb heat for a few seconds, and push it back in when the shaking gets worse.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It gets worse and she flinches and shoves it back in. Abby reaches across, pulls the knob all the way out again, and keeps two fingers on it. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"It gets worse first. That's how you know it's working."</span>`,
          why:`Right tool, wrong amount of patience. When the ice melts, the water goes through the engine, and it runs rougher for a while. Back out of it and the ice wins. Full heat, and leave it on until the power's back.`},
        {t:`Move the fuel selector to LEFT, in case it's a fuel problem from one tank.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Her hand drops toward the red handle on the floor. Abby's knee gets there first. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"You want to wrestle Biscuit's selector right now? What changed in the last five minutes?"</span>`,
          why:`What changed was a long descent at low power on a cool, wet morning. That's carb ice until proven otherwise. And moving a sticky selector in flight is how you invent a second problem.`},
        {t:`Pull full carb heat, leave it on, and keep flying while it runs rougher for a bit.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She pulls the knob all the way out. The RPM sags another hundred and the shaking gets worse, much worse, for maybe twenty seconds that feel like a week, and she keeps her hand off the knob and holds altitude because that's the only job she has. Then it smooths out, all at once, like somebody flipped a breaker, and the tach needle climbs. She breathes out. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Carb ice,"</span> Abby says. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"In May. At thirteen degrees. Now you'll never forget it."</span>`,
          why:`Fixed pitch prop, RPM loss, then roughness, on a humid day after low power. Full carb heat, all the way, and leave it. It gets worse as the ice melts and then it gets better. Next time, carb heat on before you pull the power back.`},
        {t:`Lean the mixture until the roughness smooths out, then fly home to Centennial.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`She reaches for the red knob. The engine gets rougher. <span class="said" data-who="Abby">"Wrong red knob. Mixture's not your problem. What did we just spend fifteen minutes doing?"</span>`,
          why:`Leaning can smooth out a too rich engine, but this engine isn't rich. It's choking on ice. Fix the cause, and the roughness goes with it.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f2s10`, title:`The next 100 hour`,
     setup:[`From Jordan's legal pad. The club tracks time in service off Biscuit's tach. The last 100 hour inspection was signed off at tach 2,341.8. This morning the tach reads 2,418.6.`,`The partnership agreement copies 91.409(b) word for word, including the rule that the 100 hours can run over by up to 10 hours, but only to fly to a place where the inspection can be done, with the extra time counted against the next interval. Jordan's lessons run about 1.4 hours of tach time each.`],
     fields:[{label:`Hours since the last 100 hour`,answer:76.8,tol:0.01},{label:`Hours left before the next one is due`,answer:23.2,tol:0.01},{label:`Tach time when it's due`,answer:2441.8,tol:0.01},{label:`Full 1.4 hour lessons Jordan could fly before then, if nobody else flew it`,answer:16,tol:0},{label:`If it's flown 3.0 hours past due to reach the shop and inspected at tach 2,444.8, the next 100 hour is due at`,answer:2541.8,tol:0.01}],
     solution:[`Since the last inspection: 2,418.6 minus 2,341.8 = 76.8 hours.`,`Remaining: 100 minus 76.8 = 23.2 hours. Due at 2,341.8 plus 100 = 2,441.8 on the tach.`,`Lessons: 23.2 ÷ 1.4 = 16.57, so 16 full lessons. A seventeenth would end at 23.8 hours, past due.`,`The overfly: the inspection was due at 2,441.8 and got done at 2,444.8, using 3.0 of the allowed 10 hours. The next interval is counted from when it was due, not from when it got done: 2,441.8 plus 100 = 2,541.8. The 3 hours come out of the next 100.`,`Remember the legal point from Walt: the law wouldn't require any of this for Jordan's lessons, because Abby doesn't provide the airplane. The club agreement does.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f2s9`, title:`Fly it or fix it`, intro:`Abby wrote six squawks on the briefing room whiteboard. No MEL for Biscuit, and assume the equipment list doesn't call any of these required unless the rules do. Decide what each one means.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`The panel clock has stopped. Day VFR lesson.`, choices:[`Grounded until it's fixed`,`Can fly after the 91.213(d) steps`,`Day VFR only, after the 91.213(d) steps`], answer:1, why:`A clock isn't on the day or night VFR list in 91.205. Deactivate it or remove it, placard it INOPERATIVE, and a pilot or mechanic determines it isn't a hazard.`},
      {prompt:`The oil temperature gauge reads zero with the engine warm.`, choices:[`Grounded until it's fixed`,`Can fly after the 91.213(d) steps`,`Day VFR only, after the 91.213(d) steps`], answer:0, why:`91.205(b) requires an oil temperature gauge for each air cooled engine. That's the second O in TOMATO. It can't be deferred.`},
      {prompt:`The landing light is burned out. Personal flight, not for hire.`, choices:[`Grounded until it's fixed`,`Can fly after the 91.213(d) steps`,`Day VFR only, after the 91.213(d) steps`], answer:1, why:`91.205(c) requires a landing light at night only when operating for hire. For Jordan it can be placarded and deferred, day or night, though you'd want it fixed before a night flight anyway.`},
      {prompt:`The left fuel gauge is stuck on full.`, choices:[`Grounded until it's fixed`,`Can fly after the 91.213(d) steps`,`Day VFR only, after the 91.213(d) steps`], answer:0, why:`91.205(b) requires a fuel gauge showing the quantity in each tank. A gauge stuck on full is worse than no gauge.`},
      {prompt:`The position lights don't work. The flight is this afternoon, landing well before sunset.`, choices:[`Grounded until it's fixed`,`Can fly after the 91.213(d) steps`,`Day VFR only, after the 91.213(d) steps`], answer:2, why:`Position lights are required at night by 91.205(c). For day VFR they can be deferred under 91.213(d), placarded, and the flight has to end before night.`},
      {prompt:`The stall warning horn doesn't sound during the preflight test.`, choices:[`Grounded until it's fixed`,`Can fly after the 91.213(d) steps`,`Day VFR only, after the 91.213(d) steps`], answer:0, why:`A stall warning is part of the 172N's VFR day type certification requirements, and 91.213(d)(2)(i) bars deferring anything the type certification requires. It gets fixed before flight.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`Carburetor ice is most likely to form when the outside air temperature and relative humidity are`, opts:[`below 70°F (21°C), with humidity above 80 percent`,`below 32°F (0°C), with humidity below 50 percent`,`above 90°F (32°C), with humidity below 30 percent`], a:0, why:`The PHAK says carb ice is most likely below 70°F (21°C) with relative humidity above 80 percent, and possible up to 100°F with humidity as low as 50 percent. Very dry air carries too little moisture.`, task:`I.G`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`In an airplane with a fixed pitch propeller, the first indication of carburetor ice would most likely be`, opts:[`a loss of RPM`,`a rise in oil temperature`,`an increase in manifold pressure`], a:0, why:`With a fixed pitch prop, the first sign is a drop in RPM, which may be followed by roughness. With a constant speed prop, it shows as a drop in manifold pressure.`, task:`I.G`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Carburetor heat is applied in an airplane with a fixed pitch propeller, and carburetor ice is present. The RPM will`, opts:[`increase at once, then hold steady`,`decrease, then remain constant`,`decrease, then gradually increase`], a:2, why:`Heated air is less dense, so RPM drops first. As the ice melts, RPM gradually rises. If there's no ice, the RPM drops and then stays constant.`, task:`I.G`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Carburetor heat normally should not be used during takeoff because it`, opts:[`leans the fuel and air mixture`,`increases the risk of carb ice`,`reduces the engine's power output`], a:2, why:`Heated air is less dense, which reduces power, up to 15 percent according to the PHAK, and it enriches the mixture rather than leaning it. Takeoff needs full power.`, task:`IV`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`After shutdown, a pilot should treat the propeller as if the engine could fire because`, opts:[`the magnetos need no power from the airplane's electrical system`,`the starter can engage without the master switch on`,`the battery keeps the spark plugs charged for several minutes`], a:0, why:`Magnetos generate their own current. If a P lead is broken, the magneto stays hot with the switch OFF, and moving the prop can start the engine.`, task:`XII`, domain:`Airport and flight`},
    {q:`If a pilot climbs from a high elevation airport without adjusting the mixture, the fuel and air mixture will become`, opts:[`too lean, causing detonation`,`excessively rich`,`correct, because the carburetor compensates`], a:1, why:`Air density drops with altitude while the fuel stays the same, so the mixture gets richer. That can cause roughness, power loss, and fouled plugs.`, task:`I.G`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`Under 14 CFR 91.203, which certificates must be in the aircraft for it to be operated?`, opts:[`Registration certificate and the latest annual inspection entry`,`Airworthiness certificate and the FCC radio station license`,`Airworthiness certificate and registration certificate`], a:2, why:`91.203 requires an appropriate and current airworthiness certificate, displayed, and an effective registration certificate. The FCC station license is needed only for international flights. Inspection records stay with the maintenance records.`, task:`I.B`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`If the static ports become completely blocked during level cruise, the altimeter will`, opts:[`read higher than actual as the airplane climbs`,`freeze at the altitude where the blockage occurred`,`drop back to show field elevation`], a:1, why:`Trapped static pressure freezes the altimeter at the altitude where the blockage occurred, and the VSI shows zero. The airspeed indicator still works, but it's wrong.`, task:`I.G`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`When an alternate static source inside the cabin is selected, the altimeter will usually indicate`, opts:[`slightly lower than actual`,`the same as before`,`slightly higher than actual`], a:2, why:`Cabin pressure is lower than outside, so the altimeter reads slightly higher than actual, the airspeed indicator reads greater than actual, and the VSI shows a momentary climb.`, task:`I.G`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A center zero ammeter shows a full scale discharge in flight, the needle pinned left of center. This indicates`, opts:[`a malfunction of the alternator`,`a malfunction of the voltage regulator`,`that the battery is fully charged`], a:0, why:`The PHAK says a full scale minus deflection means the alternator or generator has malfunctioned, and a full scale plus deflection means the voltage regulator has. Either way, follow the POH.`, task:`I.G`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`For VFR flight during the day, 14 CFR 91.205 requires which item in a fixed gear airplane with an air cooled engine?`, opts:[`An attitude indicator`,`An oil temperature gauge`,`An electric landing light`], a:1, why:`91.205(b) requires an oil temperature gauge for each air cooled engine. An attitude indicator is an IFR item, and a landing light is required only at night when operating for hire.`, task:`I.B`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`The clock in an airplane with no MEL is found inoperative before a day VFR flight. It isn't required for the flight. The pilot may take off if the clock is`, opts:[`deactivated or removed and placarded inoperative`,`written up in the maintenance records after the flight`,`signed off by an A&P on a special flight permit`], a:0, why:`Under 91.213(d), the item must be removed or deactivated and placarded INOPERATIVE, and a pilot or mechanic must determine it isn't a hazard. A special flight permit is for airplanes that can't meet airworthiness requirements.`, task:`I.B`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`An airplane's last annual inspection was completed on July 15 last year. Without another annual, it may be operated through`, opts:[`July 15 of this year`,`July 31 of this year`,`August 31 of this year`], a:1, why:`91.409(a) requires an annual within the preceding 12 calendar months. Calendar months end on the last day of the month, so it's good through July 31.`, task:`I.B`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`A flight school rents its airplane with its own CFIs. The 100 hour is due at 1,450.0 hours. The airplane is flown to 1,456.0 to reach the shop, where it is inspected. The next 100 hour is due at`, opts:[`1,556.0 hours`,`1,550.0 hours`,`1,560.0 hours`], a:1, why:`The 100 hour limit can be exceeded by up to 10 hours only to reach a place where the inspection can be done, and that excess counts against the next interval. Next due is 1,450.0 plus 100.`, task:`I.B`, domain:`Preflight`},
    {q:`The batteries in a required ELT must be replaced or recharged when the transmitter has been used for more than`, opts:[`30 cumulative minutes, or after 75 percent of their useful life`,`1 cumulative hour, or after 50 percent of their useful life`,`1 cumulative hour, or 12 calendar months after installation`], a:1, why:`91.207(c) says batteries are replaced after more than 1 cumulative hour of use or when 50 percent of their useful life has expired. The 12 calendar month rule is for the ELT inspection, not the battery.`, task:`I.B`, domain:`Preflight`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `Abby wrote up the lesson in the briefing room, 1.4 dual, and in the remarks put CARB ICE RECOGNITION AND RECOVERY, which made it sound much calmer than it had been. Jordan said she'd be a minute and walked back out to Biscuit.`,
    `She sat in the left seat with the door open and took the green composition notebook out of her bag. She'd been reading it a few entries at a time, rationing it. She flipped forward to see if he'd ever written about this. He had.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Danny's logbook, a page from June, seven years ago">June 3. KAPA local, 1.2. Pretty morning, 64 and wet after a storm. Long descent to Front Range, throttle back, admiring the view like a tourist. Added power, engine ran like a blender full of gravel. Pulled carb heat. It got WORSE. Which the book says it does. Which I forgot for the three longest seconds of my life. Then smooth as butter. Ice. In June. In Colorado. Carb heat on before every power reduction from now on. Marisol says she told me so. Marisol has told me so about everything.</prop>`,
    (s.score>=27
      ?`Jordan laughed out loud, alone in the airplane, and then had to sit for a second with her hand over her mouth. Same airplane, same descent, same blender. He'd been scared exactly the way she'd been scared, and he'd written it down so somebody would know.`
      :`Jordan laughed out loud, alone in the airplane, and then had to sit for a second. Some of the last week had gone sideways, and she knew which parts. He'd been scared exactly the way she'd been scared today, and he'd written it down so somebody would know.`),
    `Back in the hangar Marisol was on the stepladder at the whiteboard. Under Danny's block capitals she was writing a new list in her own small neat ones, every item Jordan had found in the logbooks, with dates and tach times. She hadn't erased a single letter of his. The ELT line had a box around it and a new date beside it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"You find anything else in there, you tell me first,"</span> she said without turning around. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"Before Walt. Before the chat. Before Russ, God help us."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Jordan" data-tone="Quiet, honest, steady.">"I will."</span>`,
    `Marisol climbed down, opened the lid of her big red toolbox, and stood looking into it for a while. Jordan could see the inside of the lid from where she was. Taped to it, between a torque chart and a faded photo of a Super Cub on skis, was a short piece of safety wire twisted into an ugly lumpy spiral. Under it, on masking tape, in Marisol's capitals: D. REYES 2017. DO NOT FLY.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"My divorce was 2017,"</span> Marisol said. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol" data-tone="Quiet, plain, grief underneath.">"Bad one. I was in here every weekend because I didn't want to be at the house. And your brother started showing up Saturday mornings with two coffees, saying he wanted to learn to safety wire. Every Saturday for a year. He was terrible at it. Worst hands I ever saw on a grown man. He never got any better, and he never stopped coming."</span> She touched the wire with one finger. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol" data-tone="Quiet, plain, grief underneath.">"Took me till about Christmas to figure out he wasn't there to learn anything."</span>`,
    `Jordan hadn't known. Nobody in the family had known. Danny had told them about Moab and dirt strips and the time he saw a bald eagle off the wingtip. He'd never once mentioned a year of Saturdays sitting on a bucket in a hangar, being bad at something on purpose so a friend wouldn't be alone.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Marisol" data-tone="Quiet, plain, grief underneath.">"He made that board so I wouldn't have to keep the list in my head anymore,"</span> Marisol said. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol" data-tone="Quiet, plain, grief underneath.">"And I let him. And then he was gone and the list just stopped."</span> She closed the toolbox lid gently. Something soft shifted inside, a padded bag of some kind, and she put her hand flat on the lid as if to keep it there. <span class="said" data-who="Marisol">"You don't read like him, you know. He never read anything. You read like somebody who'd have caught it."</span>`,
    `Jordan drove home with the strip of blue tape still stuck to her kneeboard. On the seat beside her the phone buzzed once.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Abby Strand" time="6:03 PM">Thursday 7am if the weather plays nice. Check the TAF before you text me. If you don't know what a TAF is, that's next week.</prop>`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch02.mp3`, len:`about 40 minutes`},
  next:`Weather School`
});
